- Chapter 11: Segmentation done right
    - Carried from Chapter 10
        - We saw averages hide structure. The standard industry move is to slice by country, age, gender. That's a start, and often misleading.
    - Inquiry loops planned
        - Loop A: simulate users with two attributes -- a demographic slice (e.g. country) and a behavioral slice (active/contributor/silent-intentional/passive-consumer). Apply the same treatment. Look at effects per demographic slice -- noisy, mostly null. Look at effects per behavioral slice -- clear and large in some, opposite in others.
        - Loop B: connect back to the wine study. The "20-yo college kids" is a demographic slice. But the right question is "who in the population responds like a 20-yo college kid did?" -- that's a behavioral characterization.
        - Loop C: how do we even build behavioral segments? Heuristics: tenure, weekly active rate, contribution rate, recency of first activity. Show three different segmentations on the same simulated population and the differing stories they tell.
        - Loop D: Bayesian hierarchical model with segments as a partial-pooling level. Compare to running the test independently per segment.
        - Big question: when subgroups disagree with the whole, sometimes they reverse the conclusion. That deserves its own chapter.
    - expkit modules used
        - expkit.segments.behavioral (NEW)
        - expkit.sim.user_segments
        - expkit.inference.bayes (hierarchical models in PyMC)
